---
// The explanation below the Lab: the data, the symbols, how k-Means works,
// choosing k and starting points, and the model in short. Static HTML;
// formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="kmeans-data">
    <h2 id="kmeans-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      The graph is a map of a park, 100 metres on each side. Each shape is a
      person. The lettered rings are ice-cream stands, and everyone walks to the
      stand nearest to them. Their colour and shape show which one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody told the model which group anyone belongs to: there are no labels.
      k-Means finds the groups on its own. The numbers are made up, and{' '}
      <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows another day in the park.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="kmeans-symbols">
    <h2 id="kmeans-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>xᵢ</dt>
        <dd>Where person i is standing.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>μⱼ</dt>
        <dd>Where stand j is. (μ is the Greek letter "mu".)</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>cᵢ</dt>
        <dd>The stand person i walks to: the nearest one.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">k</dt>
        <dd>How many stands, so how many groups.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-error">J</dt>
        <dd>
          Every walk, squared, added together. Smaller is better. The typical
          walk, √(J ÷ n), turns it back into metres.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="kmeans-how">
    <h2 id="kmeans-how">How k-Means works</h2>
    <p>Start with k stands anywhere. Then repeat two moves:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Group:</strong> every person goes to their nearest stand.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.assign} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Move:</strong> every stand moves to the middle of its people,
        the average of their positions. The dashed arrows show where each stand
        will go.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.update} />
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Moving a stand to the middle of its group always shortens that group's
      walks, and regrouping never makes anyone walk further. So the total J
      shrinks with every step, until nothing changes: the stands have settled.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.objective} />
    <p>
      Press <strong>Step</strong> to watch one move at a time, or{' '}
      <strong>Run</strong> to let it finish. Drag a stand anywhere, and the
      groups change at once.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="kmeans-k">
    <h2 id="kmeans-k">Choosing k, and where to start</h2>
    <p>
      More stands always mean shorter walks, so J alone cannot pick k: with one
      stand per person, nobody walks at all. Look instead for the k where adding
      a stand stops helping much. Here the park has three busy spots, so the
      typical walk drops a lot from 2 to 3 stands, and only a little from 3 to
      4.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the stands start matters too. k-Means only ever improves the stands
      it has, so a bad start can settle on a poor answer, for example one stand
      stuck between two groups. Drag the stands somewhere new and run it again
      to see a different result. In practice, k-Means is run from several starts
      and the run with the smallest J is kept.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="kmeans-summary">
    <h2 id="kmeans-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Choose k</strong> and place k stands.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Group</strong> every point with its nearest stand.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Move</strong> every stand to the middle of its group.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Repeat</strong> until nothing moves: J is then as small as these
        starting stands allow.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
